title: Store tables
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The key-value store's tables, primary and derived, and how their keys are built.

The store is a set of LMDB tables, each an ''ordered map of byte strings'': a read is
a lookup or a scan of a key range. Everything below is designed around that — keys
are built so that what a read needs together is one contiguous range. Each table
holds one kind of thing under one form of key, and its name says what (November
2026: the tables were gone over one by one — kinds told apart by a first byte became
tables of their own, and the values got a compact encoding).

!! How keys are built

* Integers are big-endian, so byte order is numeric order.
* A uuid is its 16 bytes.
* A name inside a composite key (a field name, a node's name) is escaped (`00` → `00 FF`) and ended by `00 00`, so that no name is a prefix of another: `tag` and `tags` never share a range.
* `·` below separates the parts of a composite key; `F` is a field name.
* Every index table starts with the field: what a query of one field reads is one range.

Values are encoded compactly: lengths and counts are varints (LEB128), a signed
integer a zigzag varint, a value type one byte, a uuid its 16 bytes with no length
([[Value encoding in the store]]).

!! Primary tables (`kvstore/mod.rs`)

What was written, which nothing else can give back. The rest of the daemon reaches it
through `Rows` and `Log` ([[The storage boundary]]).

|!Table |!Key |!Value |!Serves |
|`version` |`primary_version`, `derived_version` |the format version of the primary tables, of the derived ones |the open: another version is converted, or derived again ([[Persisted format changes]]) |
|`global_primary_state` |a name |a number (below) |the counters and pointers of the primary data |
|`metarecords` |dense id |the record: its fields and values; then, if a pending write names it, when its fields and values were last written by hand |a record's rows, in one read |
|`ids` |uuid |dense id (u32) |a metarecord's dense id; the reserved ids too |
|`live_metarecords` |chunk |a roaring bitmap of dense ids |the metarecords that exist ([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]) |
|`pending_writes` |part · write number |kind · content · the ids of the part |the writes to many records not yet written into the records, deletions included ([[Mass writes in the store]]) |

The log's tables are primary too ([[Log storage]]):

|!Table |!Key |!Value |!Serves |
|`log_entries` |first op id |log context · timestamp · origin · effect · file action · first reverted op · parameters · target count |the log, in order ([[The operation log]]) |
|`log_targets` |first op id · chunk number |the chunk's targets (uuids), then its before |which metarecords an entry wrote, and what to undo it with |
|`log_entries_by_context` |log context · first op id |target count |an undo or revert by context |
|`log_reverted_by` |first op id of the reverted entry · first reverted · last reverted · first op id of the revert |— |whether an operation still stands ([[The undo selection]]) |
|`log_watcher_entries`, `log_user_entries` |first op id |— |the two retention classes ([[Automatic retention]]) |
|`log_contexts` |context id |name · description · parent · created |[[Log contexts]] |
|`log_context_children` |parent · child |— |the contexts' nesting |
|`log_context_reverts` |`0` · reverting · reverted, and `1` · reverted · reverting |`1` if the whole context was reverted |what a context reverts and what reverts it, without reading entries ([[Log context summaries]]) |

!!! The record

A record's rows are ''one key'', its dense id, in `metarecords`: reading a record is
one read, and so is writing it, whatever its fields — a real file carries a dozen
`mfr_*` and metadata fields. The price is that a read of one field reads the others
with it. A row has no key of its own and no id.

```
record = varint field count · { field }
         [· extras]                       only while a pending write names it
field  = varint len · name · nothing
       | varint len · name · type · varint value count · { value }
```

The type is written once per field, not per value: a field holds `nothing` or values,
never both ([[Nothing versus unknown]]). `tags = "foo"` is 12 bytes, ten tags 48.

Every metarecord has a record, an empty one included: existence is one case. A
deleted metarecord's record stays until a reindex: it says which index entries are
filed under its id, which a restoration needs ([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]).

The ''extras'' say when a field or a value was last written by hand, so that an older
pending write does not touch it ([[Mass writes in the store]]):

```
extras = varint count · { field · write number }                  fixed fields
         · varint count · { field · type · value · write number } fixed values
```

!!! `global_primary_state`

Each key is a name, each value one big-endian integer. A key that is absent reads as
its default.

|!Key |!Holds |!Written by |!Read by |
|`log_head` |the id of the last operation in the log; absent, an empty log |every write, an undo included |the log's readers: HEAD ([[The operation log]]) |
|`next_log_op` |the next operation id — never reused, an entry taking as many as it has targets |every write |the next write |
|`next_log_context` |the next log context id, from 1, never reused |a context's creation |the next one ([[Log contexts]]) |
|`watcher_log_context` |the context the watcher's writes hang under |the watcher's first write, or its first after that context was pruned |each watcher write ([[Automatic retention]]) |
|`next_write_number` |the clock of the writes: the next number of a pending write, a fixed field or value, a pending index write |a pending write's entry, a write to a record one names, a pending index write |their order ([[Mass writes in the store]]) |
|`next_reindex` |how many times the dense ids were numbered |each reindex |a page's cursor, refused if made under another numbering ([[Pagination and sorting]]) |

`next_write_number` is one clock for everything ordered against the pending writes,
never set back. It is not the operation id: a write needs its number before the log
gives it an id, one operation can make several pending index writes of a field, and
some writes have no operation (a record given back, a fold).

!! The dense numbering

Each metarecord has a small integer, its ''dense id'', and bitmaps hold these ids,
not uuids. The numbering is ''primary data'': `metarecords` is filed under it and
`live_metarecords` holds it, so it cannot be derived again — a reindex ''renumbers''
(`ids`, `live_metarecords`, the keys of `metarecords`) and then rebuilds the indexes.

Ids are handed out in discovery order and never reused between two reindexes. A
metarecord deleted and created again (a revert) keeps its id. An id of `ids` outside
`live_metarecords` is ''deleted'' if it has a record, ''reserved'' if not: given to
the parent of a position before that parent is created, which takes it when it
comes. Whether an id is reserved is one read, at a creation or a restoration only.

Moves and late discoveries scatter a subtree's ids over the years; `mf repo reindex`
gathers them again ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]). It numbers the
metarecords in ''forest order'' — each forest walked depth first, a folder's
children by name, the forest of the most positions first — then those in no forest,
in discovery order. A record comes at its first position, so after a reindex every
subtree of the largest forest is one run of ids. What a client holds is refused
afterwards: a page's cursor is bound to `next_reindex`.

!! Derived tables — the indexes (`kvstore/derived.rs`)

Computed from the primary tables, read by the query evaluator through `KvSource`;
`mf repo reindex` rebuilds them all ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]).

|!Table |!Key |!Value |!Serves |
|`global_derived_state` |a name |a number: `next_metarecord_id` (the largest id of `ids` plus one), `log_entries_by_metarecord_indexed_up_to` |the next dense id; how far the log is indexed by metarecord |
|`uuids` |dense id ÷ 4096 |the uuids of those 4096 ids, 16 bytes each — the nil uuid for an id with none —, 64 KiB always |the inverse of `ids`: one key for 4096 consecutive ids. A query's answer, a write one record at a time, the log's chunks by dense id read their uuids range by range, and a write puts each range it changed once, at its commit. Cold, on 400 000 records, the uuids of 4096 ids spread over the store took ~90 ms against ~400 ms one key per id, one uuid alone 0.9 ms against 1.6 and 110 ns against 620 warm; a creation alone, committed, ~1 ms more for its range (November 2026, `tests/probe_uuid_reads.rs`). So no metarecord has the nil uuid |
|`field_types` |F · type |how many rows of that type (`nothing` not counted) |a field's type ([[One value type per field name]]); a key goes at 0 |
|`index_value_holders` |F · value key · chunk |bitmap |the holders of a value — every value has its own, `nothing` included; a position's value is its name; a `ref`'s holders are its referrers ([[Sorting and postings]]) |
|`index_value_counts` |F · bit · chunk |bitmap |how many values (not `nothing`) each id holds, one set per bit (below) |
|`index_parent_holders` |F · parent's dense id · chunk |bitmap |a node's children ([[The forest in the store]]) |
|`index_raw_holder` |F · parent's dense id · name |the child's dense id |a child by name: path lookup, a folder by name |
|`index_descendants` |F · node's dense id · chunk |bitmap |every id below a node, itself excluded |
|`index_parents` |F · chunk |bitmap |the nodes with a live child: leaves set aside |
|`index_trigram_texts` |F · trigram · 0 |the texts holding the trigram: per text, its partition, length, key |a trigram kept by value ([[Text search in the store]]) |
|`index_trigram_holders` |F · trigram · chunk |bitmap |the ids holding a trigram kept by metarecord |
|`index_trigram_state` |F |by_ids |whether a trigram of the field is by metarecord |
|`index_long_texts` |F · chunk |bitmap |the ids whose text is too long for trigrams |
|`index_histogram_rows` |F · bucket |rows (count) |the planner's estimate of a range ([[Query planner]]) |
|`index_histogram_keys` |F · bucket |posting keys (count) |the cost of walking it |
|`index_pending_writes` |F · chunk · write number |kind · content · the ids of the chunk |a write to many records' values not yet in the postings ([[Mass writes in the store]]) |
|`log_entries_by_metarecord` |uuid · op id |— |a metarecord's history; written at the idle fold, the entries past the pointer read directly ([[Log storage]]) |

A key of an index begins with its field. The tables that were kinds of one table —
the sets, the trigrams — kept at first the byte that told the kinds apart at the head
of their keys, and `index_histogram_keys` the `FF` that told its keys from the rows'
after the field, a byte per key for nothing; both went in November 2026 (derived
version 25): the code names the table it reads.

A ''set'' is a bitmap cut in chunks of 65 536 ids (the high 16 bits), one key per
chunk: a write rewrites one chunk, a read unions a few. The files of one folder
mostly get consecutive ids, so a folder's bitmaps compress to a few runs. A deleted
metarecord's id leaves `live_metarecords` and stays everywhere else, so every answer
is read through it ([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]).

The aspects of a position ([[Field aspects]]) are its tables: `:value`, the name, is
in `index_value_holders`; `:parent` in `index_parent_holders`; `:raw`, the parent and
the name, in `index_raw_holder` — a single child, names being unique among a node's
children. `index_parents` is an optimisation only: a node is in it when its children
in `index_parent_holders` meet `live_metarecords`.

!! The count of values

How many values of a field a record holds is kept as ''one set per bit of the
count'': a record holding five tags (binary 101) is in the sets of bits 0 and 2. Two
questions asked all the time are read off them — a record ''holds a value'' when it
is in any of the sets, and ''holds several'' when it is in any but the first (what a
sorted walk needs, [[Sorting and postings]]) — and so are the schema check's "more
than, fewer than so many values" ([[Schema]]), by comparing the counts with the
bound bit by bit from the highest, without reading a record. The bits of a field are
read by one scan of its prefix.

Why a count and not two flags: a write to many records at once can keep a count
without reading any of them. Adding a value to the records that lack it is ''+1'' on
a set of ids, which on these bitmaps is a carry — a few set operations whatever the
number of ids — and removing one a borrow. A `nothing` is not a value: it is a posting
of its own in `index_value_holders`, its key the type byte alone. The ids that have
the field are those of a count above 0 and those of `nothing`.

Every row change goes through one primitive (`derive_row`), every metarecord creation
and removal through two others (`derive_created`, `derive_removed`) — reverts
included —, so the indexes are maintained in each write's own transaction; `mf repo
check` holds all of them to a rebuild, on the ids of the metarecords that exist.

!! Key size

LMDB refuses a key over 511 bytes, and a posting's key (holding a value) or a
`index_raw_holder` key (holding a name) can hold any text:

* ''A text over 256 escaped bytes'' is keyed by that prefix, a marker `00 01` and a 64-bit hash of the whole. The marker sorts after the `00 00` ending a text equal to the prefix and before anything longer, so order is kept — except among long texts sharing the prefix, which a reader resolves from the values.
* ''A node name over 300 bytes'' is keyed by its first 300 bytes and a hash, and read back from its record.
* ''A field name'' is part of every key: one over about 200 bytes cannot be written.
